pra: 0169 - boot milestones: [+Ns] stamps at probe, GPT, rootdev, pralog start

DRAFT (not committed; produced by the eMMC speed-work agent). STACKS ON
0003 + 0004 + 0005. Generated against the reconstructed CI-built tree
(pristine xnu-8796.141.3 + 0001..0165 with pre-skips
0031/0041/0097/0099/0127/0129, GNU patch -p1 --fuzz=0, index lines
stripped). Verify with GNU `patch -p1 --fuzz=0 --dry-run`, NOT git apply.

PROBLEM: no ring has ever carried wall-clock time. The stats lines tick
every 8 MiB but nothing says WHEN; the pre-logo phase (everything before
the framebuffer console) is 5-11 min after a 14 GiB restage and completely
uninstrumented (WORKLOG section 15), so the boot-time budget cannot be
split into pre-logo vs IO vs post-logo. One tiny print per milestone turns
every future ring into a timeline.

CHANGES: pra_emmc_milestone(what) prints "[+%3llu s] <what>", dividing
mach_absolute_time() by 1920000. Provenance of the constant: ADT
timebase-frequency = 1920000 (docs/pra-address-overrides.md, confirmed in
live dmesg; the same constant the busy-note rate limit divides for its
10 s window). mach_absolute_time starts near kernel entry, so the value is
seconds since kernel start - exactly the boot timeline. Four call sites:

  1. pra_emmc_map_host, after the probe prints and boot-arg parses
     ("eMMC host probed");
  2. pra_emmc_find_root_partition, after the GPT geometry print
     ("eMMC GPT found");
  3. pra_emmc_rootdev, after the /dev/sdhcroot0 ready print
     ("root device ready");
  4. pra_log_start, after the PRA LOG lba print ("pralog started").

Each call site runs at most once per boot (map_host returns early on
re-probe, pra_log_start once enabled), so no rate limiting. Re-probe of
the GPT path is the one exception and would print a second, later stamp -
itself useful (it timestamps the re-probe).

DEFAULT BOOT: four one-line prints. No behaviour change.

